{"id":"n-20261024-o2qwki","category":"business","display_title":"Israel Weighs Crypto for Sanctions","display_title_he":"ישראל שוקלת קריפטו נגד סנקציות","short_summary":"Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu suggested that Israel explore blockchain and cryptocurrencies as a workaround for potential international banking sanctions.","english_level":"C1","words":450,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"Globes","domain":"globes.co.il","url":"https://www.globes.co.il/news/article.aspx?did=1001560614","title":"האם קריפטו יציל את ישראל מסנקציות? השאלות מאחורי דברי נתניהו","published_at":"2026-10-24T05:37:00.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-24T05:37:00.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-24T05:49:04.314Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu recently raised the possibility of economic and banking sanctions against Israel, suggesting that the nation must organize an alternative economy. According to reports, he noted that blockchain is among the alternatives being examined and stated that he has already requested a plan on the matter. This discussion emerges amid growing concerns regarding potential international restrictions and how nations might adapt when their access to traditional financial systems becomes constrained.","Analyzing the proposal, financial analysts distinguish between two potential objectives: establishing a localized backup channel for payments during minor banking disruptions, or constructing a comprehensive infrastructure to maintain global trade under severe isolation. While technology theoretically permits digital asset transfers without relying entirely on traditional banks, money movement is only part of a much larger challenge. A country still requires foreign suppliers, financing, and willing counterparties to conduct commerce. Furthermore, experts emphasize that using cryptocurrency does not bypass existing legal restrictions or render a prohibited transaction lawful.","Several countries have already attempted to navigate similar challenges using digital assets. Russia, for example, authorized the use of digital currencies for certain foreign trade payments and developed a ruble-pegged stablecoin known as A7A5 to settle transactions, such as oil deals, without relying on Western systems or the SWIFT network. Similarly, Iran has explored digital assets over the years. Nevertheless, these models face severe liquidity constraints and remain vulnerable to Western enforcement, legal limitations, and secondary penalties. In practice, regulatory bodies and financial intelligence firms utilize sophisticated tracking tools to monitor blockchain movements, and Western authorities have even sanctioned major cryptocurrency exchanges like HTX for servicing restricted payment networks.","Moreover, transitioning toward a crypto-based financial ecosystem carries significant diplomatic and legal risks. Foreign banks and global financial institutions maintain strict compliance and risk policies. If a country attempts to evade restrictions using blockchain infrastructure, international institutions might fear legal exposure and regulatory penalties, ultimately cutting off ties with that nation's financial entities. Consequently, the core challenge is not merely technological, but heavily dependent on international trust, legal certainty, and market willingness.","Separately, regulatory developments within Israel are gradually shaping the digital asset landscape. In July 2026, the Banking Supervision department published draft updates regarding payment services tied to virtual currencies, proposing that banks should not refuse payment services solely because the activity originates from virtual currencies, provided necessary risk assessments are met. However, officials clarify that this regulatory process is intended to manage money laundering and terrorist financing risks while expanding legal services, rather than establishing a full alternative financial system for international sanctions scenarios. Ultimately, while digital assets may offer a supplementary backup channel, they cannot fully replace traditional international banking relationships or guarantee protection against comprehensive economic sanctions."],"facts":[{"fact":"Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu stated that Israel should prepare for potential banking and economic sanctions by developing workaround economic options, including blockchain.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Netanyahu mentioned that he requested a plan regarding alternatives involving blockchain technology.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Other countries, such as Russia and Iran, have explored or utilized digital assets and stablecoins like A7A5 to cope with financial restrictions and bypass systems like SWIFT.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Experts note that while cryptocurrency can bypass traditional banks for simple transfers, it does not exempt transactions from international sanctions laws or guarantee that foreign vendors will accept payments.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Global regulatory bodies and enforcement agencies actively monitor blockchain transactions, and Western nations have imposed sanctions on crypto exchanges like HTX for aiding restricted networks.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Prime Minister","lemma":"prime minister","pos":"noun","he":"ראש ממשלה","name":""},{"text":"Benjamin Netanyahu","lemma":"benjamin netanyahu","pos":"name","he":"בנימין נתניהו","name":"true"},{"text":"recently","lemma":"recently","pos":"adverb","he":"לאחרונה","name":""},{"text":"raised","lemma":"raise","pos":"verb","he":"העלה","name":""},{"text":"possibility","lemma":"possibility","pos":"noun","he":"אפשרות","name":""},{"text":"economic","lemma":"economic","pos":"adjective","he":"כלכלי","name":""},{"text":"banking","lemma":"banking","pos":"adjective","he":"בנקאי","name":""},{"text":"sanctions","lemma":"sanction","pos":"noun","he":"סנקציות","name":""},{"tex